import LegalPage from '@/components/LegalPage.astro';
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy"
  description="How Urban Safari collects, uses, and protects the information our players and customers share with us."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  updated="Last updated · October 21, 2026"
>
  <p>
    itsybits (“we”, “our”, “us”) builds and operates the Urban Safari app.
    This policy covers what we collect when you play, why we collect it,
    and what we will (and won't) do with it. Written in plain English
    because legalese serves nobody.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>

  <h3>Your email</h3>
  <p>
    Collected at sign-in so we know it's you and so your team can function
    as a team. That's it.
  </p>

  <h3>Photos and videos you submit</h3>
  <p>
    When you complete a photo or video challenge, we store the media with
    your team. Teammates and hosts can see it; that's the point.
  </p>

  <h3>Usage data (via PostHog)</h3>
  <p>
    We use PostHog to see how the app gets used. This data is linked to a
    team identifier (something like “team_” plus a number), not to your
    name or email. We don't use it to identify you personally. It isn't
    anonymous, though, because it's tied to your team's activity.
  </p>
  <p>We track:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Which screens you visit in the app</li>
    <li>Which features you use</li>
    <li>App lifecycle events (open, close, foreground, background)</li>
    <li>Which organization and game you're playing, and how your team joined</li>
    <li>
      Challenge activity: which challenge and what type, the points earned,
      and whether an answer was correct (never the answer itself)
    </li>
    <li>Upload sizes and durations, and the app version</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We never send answer text, email addresses, phone numbers, rejoin
    codes, or photo and video content in these events.
  </p>
  <p>
    PostHog stores this data in the United States. We don't use cookies for
    it. The identifier lives in your device's local storage.
  </p>
  <p>It helps us figure out what's working and what's busted.</p>

  <h3>Error data (via Sentry)</h3>
  <p>
    When something breaks, we collect an error report so we can fix it.
    That includes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Device or browser type, and OS version where available</li>
    <li>App version</li>
    <li>Error logs and stack traces</li>
    <li>
      Performance traces (how long things take) for about 10% of sessions
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In our native apps, error reports also include your IP address. On the
    web, we don't collect IP addresses, cookies, or request bodies.
  </p>

  <h2>Session recordings and diagnostics</h2>
  <p>
    On top of the usage data above, the web player app collects a few
    diagnostics. They help us see where people get stuck and why things
    break.
  </p>

  <h3>Session recordings</h3>
  <p>
    PostHog records replays of what happens on screen in the app: the
    screens you see and where you tap.
  </p>
  <p>
    All text inputs are masked, so what you type is not recorded. That
    includes your typed answers and captions. Photos and videos are not
    recorded either.
  </p>
  <p>Replays are kept for 30 days.</p>

  <h3>Clicks and heatmaps</h3>
  <p>
    We track taps and clicks to build heatmaps of where people tap. That
    includes “dead clicks,” where you tap something and nothing happens.
    It points us to buttons that are confusing or broken.
  </p>

  <h3>Performance data</h3>
  <p>
    We measure how fast pages load and respond (often called “web
    vitals”), so we can spot slow spots.
  </p>

  <h3>Console logs</h3>
  <p>
    We capture the app's console log messages. These are the technical
    notes the app writes about itself while it runs, and they help us
    track down bugs.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>To run the game.</strong> Your email and media power core gameplay.</li>
    <li><strong>To improve the app.</strong> Usage data and recordings tell us what to build next.</li>
    <li><strong>To squash bugs.</strong> Error reports, recordings, and logs help us keep your day smooth.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>What we don't do:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Sell your personal information. Ever.</li>
      <li>Use your data for advertising or cross-app tracking.</li>
      <li>Share your information with data brokers.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Where it lives</h2>
  <p>
    Your data is stored with Supabase, a reputable cloud database provider.
    We use standard technical and organizational safeguards to keep
    unauthorized people out.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>
    As long as we need it to run the service. Photos and videos are
    retained for the duration of your game event. Session recordings are
    kept for 30 days. You can ask us to delete your data at any time. See
    contact info below.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party services</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Supabase</strong> · database and file storage</li>
    <li><strong>PostHog</strong> · product analytics, session recordings, and heatmaps (US cloud)</li>
    <li><strong>Sentry</strong> · error tracking, crash reporting, and performance monitoring</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each has its own privacy policy governing how they handle data.</p>

  <h2>Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    Urban Safari is not for kids under 13, and we don't knowingly collect
    their information. If you think we have, tell us and we'll fix it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>You can always:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask to see the personal data we have about you</li>
    <li>Ask us to correct anything inaccurate</li>
    <li>Ask us to delete it</li>
    <li>Withdraw your consent at any time</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Email us at the address below and we'll handle it.</p>

  <h2>Policy changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we update this policy, we'll post the new version here and update
    the “last updated” date at the top.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy questions or data requests: <a href="mailto:mike@urbansafari.app">mike@urbansafari.app</a>
  </p>
</LegalPage>
